Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 11:09:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(portfolio): the job pages price the job and name what
 goes wrong
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

After the guides, the nine /portfolio/[slug] pages were the thinnest content
left — 237 words median — sitting at the end of the most persuasive click
path the site has. Someone looked at a photograph of a floor and asked to see
the job, and arrived at a spec list.

Same method as the city hubs: derive, do not invent. Each entry already
states its scale in `specs` ("1,850 sq ft", "32 steps", "Matched 28 treads")
and its municipality, which is enough to price the job with the estimator
that prices everything else. The scale is PARSED OUT of the spec list the
page displays rather than stored a second time, so a number derived from the
list cannot disagree with the list the reader can see above it.

Forest Hill lands at $25,217-$35,773 for 1,850 sq ft plus 28 steps in
Toronto; Oakville at $16,490-$23,393 for 32 steps; Richmond Hill at
$6,041-$8,570 for 1,100 sq ft. Every band sits inside its service's published
per-unit range and every one is reproducible in /estimate with the same
inputs, which the test asserts against calculateEstimate directly rather than
against a snapshot.

Four entries get no number at all. The medallion, the restaurant, the
water-damage repair and the deck describe themselves by method — "Insurance
coordination", "Hidden fasteners" — not by scale, and the test asserts they
get none. Inventing a size to fill the slot is exactly what this repository
keeps being tempted by and keeps refusing. Where a number exists but does not
cover everything, the page says so: the medallion's install is priced, the
medallion is not, "because an inlay is not priceable by the square foot".

Each page also names the two most relevant failure modes for its shape of
work, quoted from the catalogue archetype that owns them with a link to the
rest, plus the local tier note and distance from the workshop. A job page
showing only the finished photograph is an advertisement; one naming what
goes wrong and how it is avoided is a specification.

273-528 words, median 413, was 195-305 median 237.

That closes the content programme. City hubs 343 -> 1,030. Guides 175 -> 552.
Job pages 237 -> 413. /faq 142 -> 721. Homepage 1,614 -> 2,193. Nothing was
invented to do it. What is left thin is /answers at 258, which is largely
correct — they are direct answers and padding them makes them worse.

344 tests pass (338 before). 393 pages, audit clean, no new URLs, no overflow
across 6 routes and 4 viewports.

